-- title:
--   The j-invariant is R-integral with residue of order -1
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ and $M' \ge 1$, and let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with residue field $\kappa = \mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$. Write $F =$ `fieldBar q M'` for the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ obtained by base change of the function field of level $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$, $H$ the kernel of $(\mathbb{Z}/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/q)^\times$, and let $F_\kappa$ be the $\kappa$-subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by the ratios $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\kappa\,p_f/\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,\kappa\,p_g$ of integral $q$-expansions of modular forms of equal weight on $\Gamma_H(q^2M')$. Let $R$ be a regular prolongation of $A$ to $F$ with residue field $F_\kappa$: a valuation subring `R.integers` of $F$ together with a surjective ring map to $F_\kappa$ whose kernel is the maximal ideal, contracting to $A$ along $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} \to F$, compatible with reduction on $A$, and such that every nonzero element of $F$ has a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-multiple that is integral with nonzero residue. Assume: $q$ lies in the nonunits of $A$; $f \in$ `R.integers` if and only if $f \cdot y = x$ in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ for some Laurent series $x, y$ over $A$ with the coefficientwise reduction of $y$ nonzero; and for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ lies in $F$, that image is $R$-integral with residue the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Then there exists an $R$-integral $j \in F$ whose underlying Laurent series lies in the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise images of the $\Gamma_0(M')$ $q$-expansion field over $\mathbb{Q}$, and whose residue in $F_\kappa$ has nonzero coefficient in degree $-1$ and lies in the $\kappa$-subfield generated by ratios of integral $q$-expansions of forms on $\Gamma_0(M')$.
--
--   The element produced is the modular invariant $j = E_4^3/\Delta$, with $q$-expansion $q^{-1} + 744 + \cdots$, viewed simultaneously as a $\Gamma_0(M')$-rational function on the full-level curve and, after reduction, as a function on the characteristic-$q$ fibre. It supplies the integral element whose powers are used in the valuation-counting arguments on the full-level model, and is cited by the computations identifying comapped level automorphisms at the supersingular locus.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_exists_jInvariant_mem_integers.lean

